## Context

At Cimory Group, recruitment data for 5 business units came from FPTK (manpower request) and sourcing files kept by 25+ recruiter PICs. Compiling it was a manual Excel process that took over 3 hours per cycle.

## Problem

The workflow relied on multiple Excel files, repetitive manual checks, and recurring data compilation. Headers, column ordering, and date formats were not consistent between files, making cross-unit tracking slow and error-prone.

## My role

Owner: I designed and built the system end to end, from analyzing the existing compilation workflow and defining validation rules to deploying the shared web application.

## As-is analysis

Before building the web system, each business unit and recruiter maintained separate tracking spreadsheets. Consolidating them required manually aligning column headers, cleaning inconsistent dates, deduplicating candidate rows, and recalculating SLA metrics every reporting cycle.

## Requirements

- Multi-source data input from 25+ recruiters across 5 business units.
- Role-based access control (RBAC) and structured upload cycle management.
- Strict schema validation with clear error reports before data enters the master database.
- Business rules for SLA calculations, duplicate handling, and data categorization.
- An audit trail so every upload and record update stays traceable.

## Solution design

Instead of solving everything at once, I rebuilt the workflow in stages as the requirements grew.

{% Steps %}
{% Step title="Excel to VBA: automating the process" %}
Automated the data compilation, standardized inconsistent headers and date formats, and added validation to catch errors earlier.
{% /Step %}
{% Step title="VBA to Python: structuring the logic" %}
Moved the processing logic to Python with Pandas for more structured validation, clearer error reports, and business rules for SLA calculations and data categorization.
{% /Step %}
{% Step title="Python to Streamlit and PostgreSQL: building the system" %}
Developed a centralized web application to replace the Excel-based compilation workflow.
{% /Step %}
{% /Steps %}

I designed the relational data structure, role-based access, upload cycles, and audit trail for multi-user data governance.

## Build

The system is built with Python, Pandas, Streamlit, and PostgreSQL. It validates incoming files through schema mapping, date and field normalization, and duplicate handling, and provides dashboards for recruitment progress, SLA monitoring, and recruiter-level performance. It covers 2,000+ FPTK records and 100,000+ sourcing candidates. Source code: [github.com/thunderclouds863/fptk-database](https://github.com/thunderclouds863/fptk-database).

## Verification

Validated the upload pipeline, schema normalization rules, duplicate checks, and SLA calculations against historical recruitment datasets and recruiter workflows prior to regular team adoption, alongside a public demo deployment (`fptkdemo-desi.streamlit.app`).

## Outcome

The system replaced a manual Excel compilation of over 3 hours per cycle and is now used regularly by the recruitment team across 5 business units.

## Reflection

Streamlit was a practical choice for rapid development and quick stakeholder iteration, while more complex multi-user workflows may eventually benefit from a decoupled backend and frontend architecture (such as FastAPI and React). Sustainable improvement also needs clear priorities and dedicated room for testing, documentation, and maintenance.
